[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"OAuth 2.0 and OpenID Connect with Spring Boot: A Comprehensive Guide"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"This tutorial provides a step-by-step guide to implementing secure authentication and authorization flows using OAuth 2.0 and OpenID Connect (OIDC) in a Spring Boot application. We will cover the fundamental concepts, configuration, and practical implementation details, including code examples."},{"order":3,"type":"heading","text":"Understanding OAuth 2.0 and OpenID Connect"},{"order":4,"type":"subheading","text":"OAuth 2.0: Authorization Framework"},{"order":5,"type":"paragraph","text":"OAuth 2.0 is an authorization framework that enables a third-party application to obtain limited access to an HTTP service, either on behalf of a resource owner or by allowing the third-party application to obtain access on its own behalf. It defines several grant types, including authorization code, implicit, resource owner password credentials, and client credentials."},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"OpenID Connect (OIDC): Authentication Layer on top of OAuth 2.0"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"OpenID Connect (OIDC) is an identity layer built on top of OAuth 2.0. It allows clients to verify the identity of the end-user based on the authentication performed by an authorization server, as well as to obtain basic profile information about the end-user in an interoperable and standard manner. OIDC introduces the concept of an 'ID Token,' a JSON Web Token (JWT) that contains claims about the authentication event."},{"order":8,"type":"heading","text":"Setting up a Spring Boot Project"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"First, create a new Spring Boot project using Spring Initializr (start.spring.io). Include the following dependencies:"},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Spring Web"},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Spring Security"},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"OAuth2 Client"},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"OAuth2 Resource Server (Optional, if you plan to protect your API)"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can also add dependencies like Thymeleaf or FreeMarker for view rendering if needed."},{"order":15,"type":"heading","text":"Configuring OAuth 2.0 Client"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"Configure your application to act as an OAuth 2.0 client. This involves specifying the authorization server's details, client ID, client secret, scopes, and redirect URI in your `application.properties` or `application.yml` file."},{"order":17,"type":"code","text":"spring:
  security:
    oauth2:
      client:
        registration:
          google:
            client-id: YOUR_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID
            client-secret: YOUR_GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET
            scope: openid, profile, email
          github:
            client-id: YOUR_GITHUB_CLIENT_ID
            client-secret: YOUR_GITHUB_CLIENT_SECRET
            scope: read:user
        provider:
          google:
            authorization-uri: https://accounts.google.com/o/oauth2/v2/auth
            token-uri: https://www.googleapis.com/oauth2/v4/token
            user-info-uri: https://www.googleapis.com/oauth2/v3/userinfo
            jwk-set-uri: https://www.googleapis.com/oauth2/v3/certs
            user-name-attribute: sub
          github:
            authorization-uri: https://github.com/login/oauth/authorize
            token-uri: https://github.com/login/oauth/access_token
            user-info-uri: https://api.github.com/user
            user-name-attribute: id"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"Replace `YOUR_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID`, `YOUR_GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET`, `YOUR_GITHUB_CLIENT_ID`, and `YOUR_GITHUB_CLIENT_SECRET` with your actual client credentials obtained from the respective OAuth 2.0 providers (Google, GitHub, etc.).  Ensure the redirect URI configured in the provider matches the default Spring Security redirect URI: `/login/oauth2/code/{registrationId}`."},{"order":19,"type":"heading","text":"Creating a Security Configuration"},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"Create a Spring Security configuration class to enable OAuth 2.0 login and configure access rules."},{"order":21,"type":"code","text":"@Configuration
@EnableWebSecurity
public class SecurityConfig extends WebSecurityConfigurerAdapter {

    @Override
    protected void configure(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {
        http
            .authorizeRequests()
                .antMatchers("/", "/public/**").permitAll()
                .anyRequest().authenticated()
                .and()
            .oauth2Login()
                .defaultSuccessUrl("/home")
                .failureUrl("/login?error=true");
    }
}"},{"order":22,"type":"paragraph","text":"This configuration allows unauthenticated access to `/` and `/public/**` paths, requires authentication for all other paths, and configures OAuth 2.0 login with a default success URL and failure URL."},{"order":23,"type":"heading","text":"Accessing User Information"},{"order":24,"type":"paragraph","text":"After successful authentication, you can access user information from the `Principal` object or using `@AuthenticationPrincipal` annotation."},{"order":25,"type":"code","text":"@Controller
public class HomeController {

    @GetMapping("/home")
    public String home(@AuthenticationPrincipal OAuth2User principal, Model model) {
        if (principal != null) {
            String name = principal.getAttribute("name");
            String email = principal.getAttribute("email");
            model.addAttribute("name", name);
            model.addAttribute("email", email);
        }
        return "home"; // Assuming you have a home.html template
    }
}"},{"order":26,"type":"paragraph","text":"This example retrieves the user's name and email from the `OAuth2User` object and adds them to the model for rendering in a view."},{"order":27,"type":"heading","text":"Implementing OAuth 2.0 Resource Server (API Protection)"},{"order":28,"type":"paragraph","text":"If you need to protect your API endpoints using OAuth 2.0, you can configure your application as a resource server.  This involves validating the access token presented by the client."},{"order":29,"type":"code","text":"@Configuration
@EnableWebSecurity
@EnableResourceServer
public class ResourceServerConfig extends ResourceServerConfigurerAdapter {

    @Override
    public void configure(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {
        http
            .authorizeRequests()
                .antMatchers(HttpMethod.GET, "/api/public/**").permitAll()
                .antMatchers("/api/**").authenticated()
                .anyRequest().denyAll();
    }

    @Override
    public void configure(ResourceServerSecurityConfigurer resources) throws Exception {
        resources.resourceId("api"); // Optional: Define a resource ID
    }
}"},{"order":30,"type":"paragraph","text":"This configuration protects all `/api/**` endpoints, allowing only authenticated requests with a valid access token.  The `/api/public/**` endpoints are made publicly accessible."},{"order":31,"type":"paragraph","text":"You also need to configure how the resource server validates the access token.  This can be done using a JWT decoder or by introspecting the token with the authorization server. For JWT, configure the `spring.security.oauth2.resourceserver.jwt.jwk-set-uri` property with the URL of the authorization server's JWK set."},{"order":32,"type":"code","text":"spring:
  security:
    oauth2:
      resourceserver:
        jwt:
          jwk-set-uri: YOUR_AUTH_SERVER_JWKS_URI"},{"order":33,"type":"paragraph","text":"Replace `YOUR_AUTH_SERVER_JWKS_URI` with the actual JWKS URI of your authorization server."},{"order":34,"type":"heading","text":"Customizing OAuth 2.0 Login"},{"order":35,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can customize the OAuth 2.0 login process by implementing custom `OAuth2UserService` and `OidcUserService` beans to handle user information retrieval and processing. This allows you to map user attributes from the OAuth 2.0 provider to your application's user model."},{"order":36,"type":"code","text":"@Service
public class CustomOAuth2UserService extends DefaultOAuth2UserService {

    @Override
    public OAuth2User loadUser(OAuth2UserRequest userRequest) throws OAuth2AuthenticationException {
        OAuth2User oAuth2User = super.loadUser(userRequest);
        // Custom logic to process user attributes
        return oAuth2User;
    }
}

@Configuration
public class OAuth2Config {

    @Bean
    public OAuth2UserService<OAuth2UserRequest, OAuth2User> oauth2UserService() {
        return new CustomOAuth2UserService();
    }
}"},{"order":37,"type":"paragraph","text":"Similarly, you can create a custom `OidcUserService` to handle OIDC user information.  Register these beans in your Spring context."},{"order":38,"type":"heading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":39,"type":"paragraph","text":"This tutorial provided a comprehensive overview of implementing OAuth 2.0 and OpenID Connect in a Spring Boot application. By following these steps, you can secure your application and API endpoints, leverage existing identity providers, and provide a seamless authentication experience for your users. Remember to consult the official Spring Security documentation and OAuth 2.0/OIDC specifications for more advanced configurations and features."}]
